Overview
Within this bounded manufacturing sample, organizational culture influences retention through what employees are willing to disclose, what digital representations they trust, whether errors can be discussed, and whether managers support knowledge work in practice. Technical constraints are relevant only where they alter trust, participation or learning behaviour. The insights are ranked by their highest graph evidence_count.
Digitally reconfigured cultural dependencies
1. Human–machine trust in AI-formalized shop-floor knowledge (governance count 52; algorithm and data-quality count 47; operational-variability count 12; infrastructure count 11).
   Conversational AI preserved maintenance knowledge in BPMN models, but stochastic and malformed outputs made expert verification culturally necessary (c101;c102). Ontologies could reconcile industrial vocabulary, yet analyst misunderstanding and expert disagreement survived formalization; digital-twin predictions likewise required organizational interpretation before informing maintenance action (c108;c124). These cases reconfigure trust: workers must neither reject automation nor defer to it unquestioningly. Retention is conditional on norms that permit contesting, correcting and contextualizing machine-produced knowledge.
2. Error-tolerant digital learning culture for AR and metaverse work (instructional count 44; usability count 9; cognitive-capacity count 4; memory-decay count 2).
   Workers without AR experience required HMD pre-training, while display confusion, fatigue and absent feedback made some participants uncertain about their performance (c104;c106). Context-rich AR reduced passive instruction, but low digital literacy could create overload in metaverse collaboration (c113;c122). Digital familiarity is therefore cultural as well as individual: experiential learning works when asking for assistance, repeating tasks and acknowledging mistakes are legitimate rather than signs of incompetence.
Persistently established cultural dependencies
1. Expert stewardship and absorptive learning in manufacturing teams (human-capacity count 18; workload and expertise count 16).
   Traditional elicitation depended on skilled analysts and domain experts who could validate captured know-how; digital twins depended on maintenance teams capable of recognizing and applying predictive insights (c108;c124). Metaverse collaboration required digital literacy, while learning-factory IoT activities disadvantaged participants without electronics knowledge (c122;c125). Retention therefore depends on a culture that values expertise, develops interpretive capacity and keeps humans responsible for validation.
2. Trust, openness and psychological safety in industrial knowledge sharing (culture and relational-climate count 17; adoption-barrier count 8).
Experts sometimes withheld knowledge or changed their behaviour when observed, weakening both interviews and the ontologies derived from them (c108;c108). SME cases linked tacit-knowledge sharing to interpersonal trust and open communication; knowledge hoarding and communication lapses produced the opposite effect (c109;c109). Digital tools can widen access, but their usefulness remains conditional on experts feeling respected and safe when exposing intuitive or proprietary knowledge.
3. Leadership-backed motivation for digital knowledge work (leadership count 10).
   Management support sustained creative knowledge-sharing environments, whereas poor strategic embedding weakened commitment and coordination around AI (c109;c111). Circular-innovation leadership encouraged experimentation and provided fault tolerance; smart-manufacturing programmes similarly required managerial buy-in (c121;c125). Motivation was sustained when leaders responded to contributions, tolerated informed failure and connected technology use to an intelligible organizational purpose.
Together, digitally reconfigured culture establishes confidence in machines and protected digital participation. Persistent culture supplies psychological safety, expertise, motivation, resources and disciplined stewardship. Their interaction converts captured or rehearsed shop-floor experience into trusted organizational memory and reusable operational capability.